From a6b9c7b7f3cad3cdd3d24625278fc9f8236cc0cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:12:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ds: add text-placeholder and tokenise code-block syntax
 colours
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two holes where colours lived outside the design system, both of which the
2026-08-17 Petrol rebrand walked straight past because nothing named them.

Placeholders had no token at all. The landing never set ::placeholder, so every
form field rendered the browser default #757575 — off-system, and the weakest
contrast on the page. Input.module.css pointed placeholders at --text-muted,
which is the same value as the input's own text colour, so an empty field was
indistinguishable from a filled one.

Adds --gray-400 #8496AC -> --text-placeholder. A new ramp step is needed because
none of the existing ones work: gray-500 measures 3.97:1 against the input
surface (rgba(255,255,255,0.04) over gray-950) and fails AA for normal text;
gray-300 is the input's value colour; gray-350 is reserved for the brand
wordmark and reads too bright. The new step measures 6.25:1 on the landing input
and 6.76:1 on a bare gray-950 surface.

Also names the two code-block syntax hues that were raw hex literals in
landing.css (.num #6EE7B7, .str #FBBF24) as --syntax-number / --syntax-string
plus --text-code-* semantics. Values are unchanged; this is naming only, so a
future accent change can reason about them instead of missing them. They
deliberately do not converge on the accent the way status colours do: inside a
code block hue is the only thing separating a number from a string, with no
icon or label to carry that distinction.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JMU8aeiUk4zVXFtYHwEcN4
